\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Ordered exterior operators span the endomorphisms}
\date{2026-11-02}

\p{On every square torus the kernel of #{d+d^\dagger} has dimension
#{4}, by the [Hodge theorem](fdip-0090). For periods at least #{3},
the [centered Dirac operator](fdip-000T) has kernel dimension
#{2^{2+e}}. That dimension equals #{4} when both periods are odd,
and it is strictly larger when either period is even.}

\p{Let #{k} be a commutative ring and let #{n} be a positive integer.
Write #{\bigwedge} for the exterior algebra of the free module on
basis vectors #{e_1,\ldots,e_n}, with basis the monomials #{e_I}
indexed by subsets #{I}, written in increasing order, and with
#{e_\varnothing=1}. Exterior multiplication and contraction along
the basis are the linear operators
##{\epsilon_i(e_I)=e_i\wedge e_I,}
##{\iota_i(e_I)=0}
when #{i\notin I}, and, when #{i} occupies position #{p} in #{I},
##{\iota_i(e_I)=(-1)^p e_{I\setminus\{i\}}.}
For subsets #{S} and #{T}, write #{\epsilon_S} and #{\iota_T} for
the compositions of these operators in increasing index order, the
largest index acting first, and write
#{M_{S,T}=\epsilon_S\circ\iota_T}. There are #{2^n} choices for
each subset, hence #{4^n} such operators, and they are a basis
of the endomorphism ring of #{\bigwedge}. In particular
#{\gamma_i=\epsilon_i+\iota_i} satisfies
##{\gamma_i\gamma_j+\gamma_j\gamma_i=2\delta_{ij}I.}
The [configuration Clifford algebra](fdip-000P) is the square
quotient of a supplied quadratic form. The operators here are
defined before any such form, and the displayed relation is the
square relation of the standard dot product. The note does not
claim that the resulting Clifford representation is injective.}

\proof{
\p{The dimension #{4} is the kernel dimension in the Hodge theorem.
The centered count is the cited formula at #{d=2}. It equals #{4}
when #{e=0}. When #{e\ge 1} the factor #{2^e} makes it strictly
larger. The two statements have the scopes already named.}

\p{The monomials are a basis, so operator identities may be checked
on #{e_I}. If #{i\in I}, then #{e_i\wedge e_I=0}. If #{i\notin I}
and #{q} indices of #{I} are smaller than #{i}, then
#{e_i\wedge e_I=(-1)^q e_{I\cup\{i\}}}. Thus #{\epsilon_i^2=0}.
For #{i\ne j} the wedge of the two basis vectors changes sign when
their order is exchanged, so
#{\epsilon_i\epsilon_j+\epsilon_j\epsilon_i=0}. A monomial contains
#{i} at most once, so #{\iota_i^2=0}.}

\p{For the contractions, take #{i\ne j} and suppose both lie in
#{I}, with positions #{p} and #{r} and with #{i<j}, so #{p<r}.
Removing #{j} first contributes #{(-1)^r}, and #{i} is still at
position #{p}, so the second removal contributes #{(-1)^p}.
Removing #{i} first contributes #{(-1)^p}, after which #{j} stands
one place earlier, and the second removal contributes #{(-1)^{r-1}}.
The two signs are opposite, and the remaining monomial is the same.
If either index is missing, both double contractions vanish. Hence
#{\iota_i\iota_j+\iota_j\iota_i=0}.}

\p{Now compare #{\iota_j\epsilon_i} with #{\epsilon_i\iota_j}. If
#{i\ne j} and #{j\notin I}, the contraction of the input is zero.
Exterior multiplication either vanishes or produces a monomial that
still omits #{j}, so the later contraction vanishes as well. If
#{j\in I} and #{i\in I}, exterior multiplication of the input
vanishes, and the contraction still leaves #{i} in place, so the
later exterior multiplication vanishes. The remaining pattern is
#{j\in I} and #{i\notin I}. Let #{p} be the position of #{j} and
let #{q} be the number of indices of #{I} smaller than #{i}.
Removing #{j} contributes #{(-1)^p}. If #{i<j}, wedging #{e_i}
back contributes the same sign #{(-1)^q} that #{e_i\wedge e_I}
carries, while the later removal of #{j} from #{I\cup\{i\}}
contributes #{(-1)^{p+1}}, because #{i} stands before #{j}. If
#{i>j}, wedging #{e_i} after the removal contributes
#{(-1)^{q-1}}, because #{j} was one of the indices counted by
#{q}, while the removal of #{j} from #{I\cup\{i\}} still
contributes #{(-1)^p}. In both subcases the two compositions land
on the same monomial with opposite signs. If #{i=j} and
#{i\notin I}, the contraction of the input vanishes and
#{\iota_i\epsilon_i} returns #{e_I}, and the product of the two
signs #{(-1)^q} is #{1}. If #{i\in I}, exterior multiplication of
the input vanishes and #{\epsilon_i\iota_i} returns #{e_I}, and the
product of the two signs #{(-1)^p} is #{1}. Therefore
#{\epsilon_i\iota_j+\iota_j\epsilon_i=\delta_{ij}I}.}

\p{The square of #{\gamma_i} is
#{\epsilon_i\iota_i+\iota_i\epsilon_i=I}. For #{i\ne j} each of
the four pairs in the expansion of
#{\gamma_i\gamma_j+\gamma_j\gamma_i} anticommutes, so the sum is
zero. This is the displayed Clifford relation.}

\p{The monomials are a free basis, so the endomorphism ring is
free on the operators that send one monomial to one monomial and
kill the rest. Order the pairs of subsets so that the cardinality
of #{T} increases, and index both the operators #{M_{S,T}} and
these monomial matrix units by that order. The coefficient of
#{e_S} in #{M_{S',T'}e_T} can be nonzero only when
#{T'\subseteq T}. A column with a strictly larger second set
therefore does not meet this row. When the cardinalities are
equal, necessarily #{T'=T}. The contraction #{\iota_T} removes
every factor of #{e_T}. Each removal meets a present factor, so
the image of the contraction is #{(\pm 1)\,1}. The following
exterior product inserts the factors in #{S'}, each into a
monomial that does not yet contain it, and the image is
#{(\pm 1)\,e_{S'}}. The coefficient of #{e_S} is therefore zero
unless #{S'=S}, in which case it is #{\pm 1}. Within one
cardinality the block is diagonal. A strictly smaller #{T'}
meets the row in an earlier column. The matrix is consequently
lower triangular, with units #{\pm 1} on the diagonal. Given a
target endomorphism, its coefficients against the #{M_{S,T}}
are the unique solution of that system: start from the empty
second set, invert the diagonal unit, and at each later row use
the coefficients already found. The operators are therefore a
basis.}
}

\p{For a single basis vector the four operators are the identity,
exterior multiplication, contraction, and their composite. On a
space of dimension #{2} any four independent endomorphisms are a
basis, and the triangular count supplies the independence.}
